/* Pass 13 D: Iroh character-driven teahouse and homeworld scenes. */
(function(){"use strict";const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const revisions={"jasmine-after-rain":{"avatar-22":{"number":22,"title":"A Place at the Table","content":"The chair had been empty all week.\n\nI didn't notice that until Mina tried to move it.\n\nThe Jasmine Dragon was between its lunch rush and the afternoon regulars, that strange hour when the shop looked empty but every cup still needed washing. Mina had a mop in one hand and a stack of accounts in the other. The chair occupied the only clear stretch between the long table and the counter.\n\n\"Can this go upstairs?\" she asked.\n\nIroh, elbow-deep in the tea tins, looked up so quickly I thought he'd heard an alarm.\n\n\"That chair stays.\"\n\nMina lowered the mop. \"It can't stay there and let me reach the storeroom.\"\n\n\"It was always there.\"\n\n\"So was the cracked floor tile. I fixed that.\"\n\nI hid a smile in my cup. The chair was ordinary, with a rubbed patch on one arm and a cushion that had been mended twice. Somebody had written an old number underneath it. I had assumed it belonged to the shop, like the kettles and the stubborn front latch.\n\nWhen Mina returned to the counter, I asked Iroh who used to sit there.\n\n\"Many people.\"\n\n\"Do they still?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\nHe answered in the soft, complete voice he used when he didn't want to answer at all. I could have let it go. I could also see Mina shifting the mop around the obstruction again.\n\n\"If you're keeping it for someone,\" I said, \"that's okay. But does it have to block everybody else?\"\n\nIroh folded a cloth over the counter. \"It belonged to an old customer who came here when the city was less kind.\"\n\n\"Did they ask you to save it?\"\n\nHe studied the worn armrest.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nI didn't press for the name. The story could belong partly to somebody who wasn't here to give permission for it to be told.\n\nMina came back with a tray of bowls. \"The carpenter can fix the loose joint for four copper,\" she said. \"I asked. If it stays downstairs, we need room around it. If it goes upstairs, the stairs are narrow. I'm not carrying it alone.\"\n\n\"Neither should you,\" I said.\n\nIroh smiled faintly. \"I have been informed that my furniture is demanding a council.\"\n\n\"Your furniture isn't demanding anything,\" Mina said. \"Your employees are.\"\n\nThat landed.\n\nIroh asked whether we could close the shop for half an hour and rearrange the table together. Mina said no: there was still an advertised tea tasting at three, and closing early would mean losing customers she'd already prepared for. We could move the chairs after the tasting, when she was being paid to work anyway.\n\nI loved that she didn't make his kindness into an excuse to work for free.\n\nThe afternoon group arrived early. A little girl with her grandmother sat at the long table, and an old porter occupied the chair beside the window. When he saw the empty seat, he asked whether he could use it.\n\nIroh opened his mouth.\n\nI watched him decide.\n\n\"Please do,\" he said.\n\nThe porter sat. He didn't become a symbol of healing. He complained about the weak tea, bought a second cup and left a respectable tip. When he rose, he thanked Mina instead of Iroh, because she was the one who had served him.\n\nLater we moved the chair closer to the others. The aisle was wide enough for Mina's cart and the tray she carried at closing. The carpenter would repair the joint on market day. Iroh paid the deposit from the shop account and wrote the amount in the ledger so she wouldn't have to chase him for it.\n\nThe carpenter arrived with a notebook and a square of dark wood for testing the joint. He asked who had the authority to approve work on the shop furniture.\n\n\"I do,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"And the clear aisle?\" Mina asked.\n\nThe carpenter looked at her. She showed him how she moved the tea cart through the room, including the turn by the kitchen door that had been blocked whenever the chair stood at its old angle.\n\n\"Then we need to measure it with the cart,\" he said.\n\nIroh stepped back to let them work. He looked almost bereaved by the empty space, though he knew the chair would return after its repair.\n\n\"Do you want to tell me whose chair it was?\" I asked.\n\n\"It belonged to someone who came to the shop when the city was less kind,\" he said. \"He liked sitting where he could see the door. I kept it there after he stopped visiting.\"\n\nI didn't ask whether the man had died or simply gone elsewhere. Iroh could decide which parts were ready to be shared.\n\n\"The people who use the shop now need the aisle,\" I said.\n\n\"I know. Sometimes I confuse preserving a memory with preserving its furniture.\"\n\nMina returned with the cart, which passed the new position without touching the table. The carpenter made a mark in his book and said he'd bring the repaired joint after market day. He named a price. Iroh paid the deposit and thanked him for checking the actual path.\n\nWhen we were alone, Iroh picked up the worn cushion.\n\n\"Do you think a person can be loved without a place being held empty forever?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" I said. \"I hope so. I've been clear that I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. I don't want anyone else's memory removed to make space for me, and I don't want a space reserved as though I have no right to leave.\"\n\nHe folded the cushion into his arms. \"That is a great deal of freedom for a chair.\"\n\n\"Your chairs are terribly political.\"\n\nHe laughed. It was a small, relieved laugh, and when he reached for my hand I was glad he had chosen to do it.\n\nI carried the cushions into the back room. He followed with the mended one.\n\n\"I liked leaving a place for people to return to,\" he said.\n\n\"You can. But they might want a different seat when they do.\"\n\nHe rubbed a thumb along the seam. \"I suppose I kept believing that if nothing changed, I could prove they hadn't been forgotten.\"\n\n\"Did it help?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\nI set the cushion down. \"I don't want to be a person you need to keep a chair empty for.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"I've told you I prefer polyamory, and that I'm ethically non-monogamous,\" I said. \"That means I may leave for visits and return. It also means I choose where I'm sitting when I get back. You don't have to freeze the shop to make room for me.\"\n\n\"I know your heart is not a reservation,\" he said.\n\n\"Good, because your reservation system is terrible.\"\n\nHe laughed. It started in his chest and worked its way into his face until I was laughing too.\n\n\"Then will you accept an invitation to dinner?\" he asked.\n\n\"With you?\"\n\n\"I was hoping.\"\n\n\"Ask me after Mina's shift ends. I want to help with the dishes we used, not all the dishes she was hired to wash.\"\n\nMina appeared in the doorway. \"That may be the most romantic thing anyone has said in this shop.\"\n\nI blushed so hard I nearly dropped the cushion.\n\nAt closing, Iroh cooked a small supper from what he'd actually bought for us. He did not raid the next day's stock to impress me. We sat at the long table, where the repaired chair would go, and he let me serve the first bowl.\n\n\"Are you comfortable?\" I asked.\n\n\"Very.\"\n\n\"Are you about to get up to help a stranger?\"\n\n\"Only if a stranger falls through the ceiling.\"\n\n\"Reasonable exception.\"\n\nHe reached for my hand across the table. When I moved closer, he asked whether he might kiss me. The kiss was slow and warm and tasted faintly of ginger. We closed the shop together and went upstairs without making the evening into a promise about all the evenings afterward.\n\nThe next morning, the chair wasn't empty.\n\nMina was sitting in it, counting the change from the till. Iroh poured her tea before she asked. Nobody suggested she give up her seat.","word_count":1358},"avatar-23":{"number":23,"title":"The White Lotus Does Not Whisper","content":"The White Lotus members arrived before the tea had finished steeping.\n\nNot late. Not early. Exactly when Iroh had said they would, which should have been my first warning that this wasn't a social visit.\n\nA woman in a travel-stained green coat set a game tile on the back-room table. Two men followed her. One of them looked toward me, then toward Iroh, as if I were a chair someone had forgotten to move.\n\n\"I can wait upstairs,\" I said.\n\n\"Do you want to?\" Iroh asked.\n\n\"No. But I would like to know whether this is a conversation I'm allowed to hear.\"\n\nThe woman stopped unfastening her satchel. \"That is a fair question.\"\n\nIroh put a fresh cup down for me. \"Jasper is my guest, not my subordinate. You may ask for a private meeting. Please do not decide that the request has already been granted.\"\n\nOne of the men gave him a look full of old battles. \"You still make everything difficult.\"\n\n\"Only the easy things.\"\n\nThe woman introduced herself as Scholar Ren. She had brought a list of travelers who needed discreet escort through Ba Sing Se, and a second list of supplies the White Lotus could not obtain without drawing the wrong attention. She did not offer me either list. I appreciated the distinction more than I expected.\n\n\"Then what can I do?\" I asked.\n\n\"Nothing, if you don't wish to,\" Ren said. \"We came to ask the general for help.\"\n\n\"I sell tea,\" Iroh said mildly.\n\n\"You also know which warehouses change their locks without announcing it.\"\n\nHis smile faded. For a moment I saw the man who had once commanded an army and later made a deliberate choice not to be that man again. Ren's hand remained on the satchel. Nobody reached for a weapon. The danger was in what the names on those papers could cost someone.\n\nI carried the kettle to the stove. Mina had left the day's stock sheet under a paperweight. Three jars of jasmine, one open tin of roasted barley, two cracked cups marked for repair. The shop could not vanish into intrigue every time an old friend needed Iroh.\n\n\"Mina is covering the morning rush alone,\" I said. \"If you need this room until noon, I can help her. If you need it until evening, we have a staffing problem.\"\n\nRen blinked. \"We can return after closing.\"\n\nIroh looked at me, and his expression softened in a way I wasn't sure the visitors noticed.\n\n\"She doesn't get paid to be part of this,\" I added.\n\n\"I beg your pardon?\" Ren said.\n\n\"Mina. Her time isn't free because the cause is important.\"\n\nAfter a long pause, Ren closed the satchel. \"After closing, then. And we'll pay for the tea.\"\n\n\"Extraordinary progress,\" Iroh said. \"Perhaps next you will ask whether I am available.\"\n\nI stayed in the shop while they arranged a later hour. Across the counter, a little boy was trying to trade three shiny buttons for a bun. Mina counted out the price and gave him a smaller roll he could actually afford, refusing Iroh's attempt to make the bakery budget disappear through generosity.\n\n\"You don't have to be kind with other people's stock,\" she told him.\n\n\"An excellent rule,\" I said.\n\n\"Then you should explain it to your boyfriend.\"\n\nI nearly dropped the tray. Iroh laughed so hard he had to sit down.\n\nRen set the folded map beside her tea and waited until Iroh finished answering Mina's last question about the till.\n\n\"Thank you for coming back after closing,\" I said.\n\n\"You weren't required to thank me,\" she answered. \"I asked for a meeting and made it inconvenient.\"\n\nIroh smiled. \"An astonishing admission from a member of the White Lotus.\"\n\nShe ignored him and looked at the map.\n\n\"The supply route crosses a bridge under inspection. We need to know whether the lower market can still receive its deliveries without using the restricted passage.\"\n\nI thought of the valley's own problems with bridges and closures. This was Ba Sing Se, not Lanternweald, but people everywhere seemed determined to imagine that a line on a drawing would make a cart fit across a damaged road.\n\n\"Who uses the path?\" I asked.\n\nRen glanced at Iroh. He nodded.\n\n\"Market workers, two relief kitchens and ordinary travelers,\" she said. \"Not everyone is part of our work. That is exactly why a mistake would matter.\"\n\nWe listed the information we could share publicly, such as the condition of the route and the time of the inspection. Names of private contacts stayed in Ren's own papers. Mina could tell us how much tea the shop needed for its real customers; she was not required to turn the stock into a private cache because a society with a flower on its tiles requested it.\n\n\"Can I help with the public delivery estimate?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" Ren said. \"You don't need the restricted names for that.\"\n\nThe boundary was almost disappointingly ordinary. I helped count the crates and noticed that one planned delivery overlapped with the shop's busiest market morning.\n\nIroh made a face. \"Mina will not thank us for that.\"\n\n\"She shouldn't have to,\" I said.\n\nRen shifted the time. When she left, she took the private list with her. Iroh still had the public schedule and an invitation to advise if he wanted. The difference had been chosen by the people doing the work rather than imposed by secrecy alone.\n\nLater, when the doors were shut and the final customer had gone, Ren came back alone. This time she asked whether I wanted to leave before she unfolded the map. I said yes to the identities, no to being excluded from every discussion about how the shop would be affected.\n\nIroh held out a chair.\n\n\"Does that mean you're joining the White Lotus?\" Ren asked.\n\n\"No,\" I said. \"It means I know where the spare lamp oil is, and I'm allowed to have an opinion about locking a public shop at the wrong hour.\"\n\nThe map showed a supply route. I helped calculate how much tea could be moved without leaving Mina short for the following week. Nothing about it made me an operative. There were portions of the plan I was not shown. I didn't insist. A boundary could be an act of care when it belonged to the people it protected.\n\nAfter Ren left, Iroh turned the White Lotus tile over in his fingers.\n\n\"I feared you would think I was keeping a second life from you,\" he said.\n\n\"You are keeping parts of a life private. That's different from making promises you can't keep.\"\n\nHe watched me carefully.\n\n\"I've told you I'm ethically non-monogamous,\" I continued. \"I prefer polyamory. I don't expect every person I care for to hand over their letters or their whole history. I do expect them to tell me when something changes our plans, our safety, or the promises we've made.\"\n\n\"You ask for honesty without ownership.\"\n\n\"I'm trying. I'm not always graceful at it.\"\n\n\"Neither am I.\"\n\nI laughed. \"That might be the first unwise thing you've said all day.\"\n\n\"Give me time.\"\n\nHe reached across the table, palm up. I laid my hand in his. For a little while he wasn't a general, a symbol, or the keeper of anybody's secret. He was tired, and a bit ridiculous, and warm beside me.\n\n\"Would you like to stay after the lamps are out?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes. But first, did you actually pay Mina for the extra hour?\"\n\nHe closed his eyes. \"I have been defeated by the greatest strategist in Ba Sing Se.\"\n\nI kissed him gently, then took the account book off the shelf. We fixed the wages before going upstairs. The curtain fell across the back room, and the rest of the night belonged to us.","word_count":1304},"avatar-24":{"number":24,"title":"A Question Worth Asking","content":"I asked Iroh who he was when nobody wanted anything from him.\n\nThe question came out while we were carrying the last dry towels to the balcony above the Jasmine Dragon. Below us, Ba Sing Se filled with evening carts and the smell of cooking oil. Someone had started singing three streets away, loudly and not particularly well.\n\nIroh looked up at the sky. \"A man who likes a quiet balcony.\"\n\n\"That's a place, not a person.\"\n\n\"A man who has brought you to a very good place.\"\n\n\"You're doing it again.\"\n\nHe took the towels from me and made a show of folding them. \"What am I doing?\"\n\n\"Answering the question that makes you sound most helpful.\"\n\nHe stopped folding.\n\nI wished, for one terrible moment, that I'd simply asked whether he wanted more tea. It would have been easier. But I wasn't falling in love with an answer to every problem. I was falling for someone who could make a joke, and make another joke, and still carry something so heavy I sometimes saw it in the way he stood up.\n\nIroh sat on the little bench. There was room beside him, but he didn't pat it. He waited.\n\n\"I used to be many things,\" he said. \"Some of them I did not choose. Others I chose badly.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I am also a man who sings off-key when he thinks no one can hear.\"\n\n\"That one's new.\"\n\n\"It is a carefully guarded secret.\"\n\nFrom the street, the unknown singer missed another note so spectacularly Iroh winced. Then he began humming the same tune and made it worse on purpose. I laughed hard enough to lean against the railing.\n\n\"You're terrible.\"\n\n\"Then I have answered at least one part of your question.\"\n\nWe sat for a while. The breeze lifted the edge of his sleeve. I noticed a tiny scorch mark from the kettle he had nearly dropped that afternoon while arguing with a supplier over the cost of dried jasmine. I touched the fabric.\n\n\"Does this happen often?\"\n\n\"Only when I am attempting to look dignified.\"\n\n\"So daily.\"\n\n\"An outrageous accusation.\"\n\nI pulled my chair closer. \"I mean it. What would you do if nobody was waiting for advice, nobody called you General or Uncle, and you could choose the afternoon for yourself?\"\n\nHe thought longer than I expected.\n\n\"I would eat something sweet that I had no intention of sharing,\" he admitted. \"I would play Pai Sho badly, so no one invited me to a serious match. I would visit a garden where nobody knew my name.\"\n\n\"Could you do any of that tomorrow?\"\n\n\"Perhaps not the garden. Mina needs me to sign the delivery order, and Lian is waiting for us to repair the hose.\"\n\n\"That's two things. Not an entire lifetime.\"\n\nHe smiled, but I could see the uncertainty beneath it.\n\n\"I've spent so much of my life being useful in order to justify the room I occupied,\" he said. \"I find it difficult to imagine occupying a room for pleasure.\"\n\nI reached for his hand, then stopped. \"Would you like me to hold it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe stayed on the balcony while a delivery cart rattled through the street below. A boy carrying buns stopped to argue with the driver about whose wheel had splashed water onto his shoes. Iroh leaned over the railing as though he might settle the matter, then drew back.\n\n\"That was very difficult,\" he admitted.\n\n\"What was?\"\n\n\"Allowing people to quarrel without offering a solution.\"\n\nI laughed. \"You can care about them without being appointed referee.\"\n\nHe sat beside me. His sleeve brushed mine, warm from the tea kettle we'd carried upstairs.\n\n\"Would you like to know something else I want?\" he asked.\n\n\"Very much.\"\n\n\"I want to be allowed to be wrong about a little thing, and not have it become evidence that I am unworthy of the life I have now.\"\n\nIt was such a quiet wish that it frightened me. I knew how many enormous mistakes stood behind his words, and how little right I had to offer absolution for harm that wasn't mine.\n\n\"I can argue with you about a burnt bun,\" I said. \"I won't use it to make a verdict on every part of your history.\"\n\nHe looked relieved, then immediately suspicious.\n\n\"You would argue about a bun?\"\n\n\"Passionately.\"\n\n\"Then you may have found your calling.\"\n\nHe stood and fetched two sweet buns from the basket we'd brought upstairs, placing them between us as though preparing for an important contest. One was slightly darker along the edge.\n\n\"Which is better?\"\n\nI took a bite of each. \"Neither. They're different.\"\n\n\"An evasion.\"\n\n\"You're the one who spent half the evening evading my questions.\"\n\nHe laughed and conceded the point. Then he asked what I wanted when nobody needed me. The question caught me by surprise.\n\n\"I want to write,\" I said. \"I want to see people I love without having to explain why loving them doesn't diminish anyone else. And sometimes I want to sit beside you without fixing anything.\"\n\nHe took my hand again. \"Then let us practice the last one.\"\n\nWe sat until the evening grew cool, sharing the buns and saying nothing important. That too was a kind of answer.\n\nHis palm was warm. It still surprised me when something that simple felt like a choice two people had made together rather than an answer one person owed.\n\n\"I'm not asking you to stop helping people,\" I said. \"But you can want things that have nothing to do with rescuing me.\"\n\n\"And what do you want?\"\n\n\"I want you to stop making me guess whether there's a person behind the wise expression.\"\n\n\"Very demanding.\"\n\n\"Extremely.\"\n\nHe turned toward me. \"Then I will tell you something selfish. When you leave to spend time with people you also love, I sometimes look at the upper room and wish you were in it. It is not a wish I intend to use against you.\"\n\nI swallowed. We'd spoken plainly about my ethical non-monogamy and my preference for polyamory. Iroh had not been asked to pretend that he never missed me. I just didn't want longing to become a debt.\n\n\"You can say you miss me,\" I told him. \"You can ask whether I'm free next week. I want to hear what you need, not discover it after you've become lonely enough to be noble about it.\"\n\nHis laugh was smaller this time. \"Noble loneliness is a remarkably foolish habit.\"\n\n\"One you've perfected.\"\n\n\"I am reconsidering my achievements.\"\n\nThe shop bell rang downstairs. Mina called that the parcel runner had delivered the repaired tea strainer and wanted a receipt. Iroh rose, then glanced at me.\n\n\"Would you mind if I took care of that?\"\n\n\"Not at all. I'll still be here.\"\n\nHe went down and came back five minutes later with a paper bag.\n\n\"Sweet buns,\" he announced. \"Bought with my own money, so the inventory will not suffer.\"\n\n\"You bought two.\"\n\n\"I have not yet learned selfishness perfectly.\"\n\nWe ate them on the bench. He got sugar on his beard. I considered telling him, then decided I liked the excuse to lean closer. I wiped it away with my thumb. He caught my wrist gently.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\" he asked.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nWe kissed until the evening voices rose and the last light slipped between the rooftops. When we broke apart, he rested his head against my shoulder for a while.\n\n\"Call me Iroh when we're alone,\" he said.\n\n\"I already do.\"\n\n\"Then keep doing it. I like the way you say it.\"\n\nBelow, the city went on needing things. Above it, the man beside me wanted another sweet bun and someone to laugh with. That was more than enough for tonight.","word_count":1298},"avatar-26":{"number":26,"title":"When Tea Goes Cold","content":"My tea had gone cold twice before Iroh noticed.\n\nThe first time, he was finding a replacement kettle for a neighbor whose handle had broken. The second, he had promised a visiting merchant that he'd explain the difference between two jasmine blends, despite Mina being perfectly qualified to do it herself.\n\nBy the time he sat across from me, the shop was nearly empty.\n\n\"You're quiet,\" he said.\n\n\"I've been waiting to talk to you.\"\n\nHe blinked. \"I'm here.\"\n\n\"I know. I've watched you pass this table six times.\"\n\nThat wasn't entirely fair. Every request had been real. The broken kettle could have burned somebody; the merchant had money to spend; the afternoon's accounts still needed signing. But I had planned to tell Iroh about an invitation to spend several days in Lanternweald, and each time I tried to start, something else became urgent.\n\nMina appeared with a stack of cups. \"I can handle the front,\" she said.\n\n\"Are you sure?\" Iroh asked.\n\n\"I'm sure because it's my shift. I would like you to stop asking me to prove it.\"\n\nHe looked chastened. I appreciated that he didn't turn her annoyance into a joke.\n\nWe moved to the back room, where rain tapped against the small window. Iroh poured another pot and let the leaves settle. I watched his hands. He had the movements of a man who could still measure time by ritual even when the rest of his day ran away from him.\n\n\"I don't want you to be my only priority,\" I said. \"I want to know when we are actually making time for each other.\"\n\n\"I thought we were.\"\n\n\"We planned this afternoon. You said yes to three more things without telling me the plan had changed.\"\n\nHis face lost some of its gentleness. \"People needed help.\"\n\n\"I know. And you chose to help them. I'm not angry that they matter.\"\n\n\"Then what are you angry about?\"\n\n\"That I became the one person you assumed would understand indefinitely.\"\n\nThe answer sat between us. I wished I could take the sharpness out of it without losing the truth.\n\nHe looked toward the door. \"I spent years trying to prove I could be of use after I'd done harm. It is easier to answer a stranger who asks for a kettle than someone who asks me to be present.\"\n\n\"Being present isn't a trick question.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"But I sometimes treat it as one.\"\n\nI told him about the Lanternweald invitation. I wanted to visit Moonroot, see the noticeboard changes, and spend time with other people I loved. He had known from the beginning that I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred polyamory. We had agreed that invitations could be accepted or declined without punishment. Still, telling him I would be away felt awkward when we'd barely been together this week.\n\nHe asked which dates I was considering.\n\n\"From next Thursday until the following Monday. I haven't confirmed them. I wanted to talk to you first because we said we would have supper Friday.\"\n\n\"Ah.\"\n\n\"I'm not asking you to make the decision for me. I'm asking whether we can move our supper to Wednesday, or make a different plan.\"\n\nHe took a breath. \"Wednesday would be lovely. I have no objection to the visit. I would have liked to hear about it before the shop closed.\"\n\n\"I was trying.\"\n\nHe winced.\n\n\"I see.\"\n\nMina came back before Iroh could rise for the kettle. She set a clean pot on the table and looked pointedly at the two of us.\n\n\"Tea is ready. Please do not create another reason for him to disappear into the storeroom.\"\n\nIroh looked offended. \"I was merely going to check whether the water was hot enough.\"\n\n\"I have spent twelve years learning that,\" Mina replied.\n\nI hid a laugh in my sleeve. She returned to the counter, leaving us a whole hour with no one asking for wisdom. The luxury of it made me uncertain what to do with my hands.\n\n\"Can I tell you what worried me about the invitation?\" I asked.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"I thought you might hear that I was going away and decide it meant I was less serious about us.\"\n\nHe considered the words instead of rushing to reassure me.\n\n\"I thought that for a moment,\" he admitted. \"Then I remembered what you told me about being ethically non-monogamous and preferring polyamory. It is not an agreement that asks you to be present every hour.\"\n\n\"Knowing that doesn't make missing me easy.\"\n\n\"No. But I am allowed to miss you without making it a condition of your return.\"\n\nI rested my hand on the table. He covered it briefly with his own.\n\n\"What would make our time here easier?\" I asked.\n\n\"Perhaps I could close the door to the back room when I've promised you an hour, and ask Mina whether she wants that shift instead of assuming she can take it.\"\n\n\"And pay her for it.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe wrote the idea in the shop schedule. Mina read it before agreeing, because she had plans of her own. The next afternoon Iroh couldn't find his favorite tea tin and spent ten minutes accusing a spoon of conspiring against him. I laughed until my face hurt.\n\nThe shop wasn't becoming less important. Neither was I. We were simply learning to distinguish a true emergency from the habit of answering every call, even when someone we loved was already sitting in front of us.\n\nWe let the rain make the next few sounds. Then I pointed at the cold tea.\n\n\"Would you mind making me a new cup?\"\n\nHe smiled, almost apologetically. \"I thought you might ask.\"\n\n\"No philosophies about whether tea is ever truly cold.\"\n\n\"I would never.\"\n\nHe started to rise. I caught his sleeve.\n\n\"Not as an escape, Iroh. You can sit while the water heats.\"\n\nHe sat again.\n\nWe made an actual arrangement. On Wednesday, Mina would close according to her agreed schedule, and we would cook our own dinner after the shop finished. If a real emergency interrupted it, we would say so. If a request could wait, it would wait. He would stop offering my free time in the name of being generous. I would speak before my resentment became a silent test.\n\n\"That's a very long list,\" he said.\n\n\"You've negotiated peace between more difficult people.\"\n\n\"None of them knew how often I forgot their tea.\"\n\nI laughed. He reached for my hand but waited until I offered it.\n\nWhen he told Mina the new arrangement, she nodded approvingly and reminded him that she would not stay late to make it happen. He thanked her, and I believed he meant it.\n\nThe new tea was hot. I drank it slowly.\n\nOn Wednesday, he kept his word. The shop closed on time. He bought mushrooms and rice on the way home and cooked without narrating a lesson about patience. We argued cheerfully over whether the mushrooms needed more salt. At the sink, I leaned against his shoulder and listened to him hum.\n\n\"Was this worth a whole afternoon of difficult conversation?\" he asked.\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\n\"Even though the tea went cold?\"\n\n\"Especially because of that.\"\n\nHe set the dishcloth aside. I asked whether I could kiss him. He said yes, and his answer had a pleased, almost bashful quality that made my chest ache.\n\nWe left the kitchen tidy enough for the morning, closed the bedroom door and kept the rest of the evening private.\n\nThe next time a merchant interrupted our planned hour, Iroh asked Mina whether she had the sale handled. She did. He came back to our table.\n\nThe tea stayed warm because we were there to drink it.","word_count":1287},"avatar-28":{"number":28,"title":"The Game We Change","content":"I beat Iroh at Pai Sho because he underestimated how stubborn I could be.\n\nHe would later claim that he had been studying an advanced strategy. I would later point out that his advanced strategy involved staring at the wrong tile for an entire minute.\n\nThe board sat between us in the Jasmine Dragon's back room. Rain had cleared the street, leaving the paving wet and dark. Mina had gone home on time. The shop's accounts were finished. For once, nobody was waiting for Iroh to be a general, a mentor or a supplier of tea.\n\n\"Your move,\" I said.\n\n\"I am considering.\"\n\n\"You've been considering long enough for the moon to change its mind.\"\n\nHe smiled and moved the white lotus tile. I took it with a move he'd shown me the week before.\n\nHis eyebrows rose.\n\n\"That,\" he said, \"was not polite.\"\n\n\"You taught me the rule.\"\n\n\"I did not expect you to remember it so enthusiastically.\"\n\nWe played on. He told me stories between turns, and I knew enough about him now to recognize when he was using one to distract me from the board. A story about a merchant who tried to sell fake jade lasted exactly as long as it took him to set up a trap on the far side.\n\nI saw the trap.\n\n\"You know,\" I said, \"telling me a story while setting a trap doesn't make the trap invisible.\"\n\n\"An observation of great insight.\"\n\n\"Stop flattering me. It gives you away.\"\n\nHe laughed and, for the first time all evening, actually looked surprised by my next move.\n\nWe had planned a relaxed game. Then a knock sounded at the rear door. Ren, the White Lotus scholar, stood in the hallway with a folded note.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" she said. \"The message arrived late.\"\n\nIroh took the note but did not open it immediately. \"Is anyone in danger?\"\n\n\"Not at present. The next meeting needs a new room.\"\n\n\"Then I will answer in the morning.\"\n\nRen nodded. She had learned to ask; he had learned that an invitation wasn't a command. Once she left, he put the note face down beside the kettle.\n\n\"Are you angry with her?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. She had a reasonable request.\"\n\n\"And you said no.\"\n\n\"Not no. Tomorrow.\"\n\nI glanced at the board. \"A very advanced move.\"\n\n\"One I have been practicing.\"\n\nWe went back to the game. He took my best piece and apologized so extravagantly that I suspected another trick. I leaned across the table to see the far corner and noticed him watching me rather than the tiles.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"You look happy.\"\n\n\"That's because I'm winning.\"\n\n\"Ah. So that is what happiness looks like.\"\n\n\"It's a rare experience for you.\"\n\nHe placed one hand over his heart. \"Cruel.\"\n\nIroh moved a tile toward the edge, then stopped with his fingertip still resting on it.\n\n\"May I change my mind?\" he asked.\n\n\"About the move?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Before you've released it, of course.\"\n\nHe drew it back. \"An excellent rule for a board game. Less useful in warfare.\"\n\n\"Fortunately we're playing Pai Sho and not conquering a city.\"\n\nFor a moment the joke went wrong. I saw the old general behind his smile and wished I'd been gentler. He looked at the pieces, then up at me.\n\n\"That is exactly why I enjoy playing with you,\" he said. \"We may correct a move without someone getting hurt.\"\n\nI let out the breath I'd been holding.\n\nHe told me about a game he'd once played against an officer who was afraid to win. Every move had been designed to flatter, and the board had become a tedious performance of loyalty.\n\n\"Did you tell him?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. I was angry that he was boring and did not ask why he was afraid.\"\n\n\"Would you act differently now?\"\n\n\"I hope so. But I can't make the past game fair simply by regretting it.\"\n\nWe played in silence for a while. I was startled by how much I wanted him to win honestly, not because he needed proof of his skill, but because I wanted to see him light up at a clever move.\n\nHe caught me watching.\n\n\"Do you intend to distract me?\" he asked.\n\n\"Possibly.\"\n\n\"An underhanded tactic.\"\n\n\"Is it working?\"\n\n\"Terribly.\"\n\nHe smiled, then caught one of my pieces. I groaned so loudly that Mina called from the stairwell to ask whether a teapot had fallen. We assured her the shop was intact.\n\nWhen we finished, Iroh offered me his hand across the board.\n\n\"Would you like to play again someday?\"\n\n\"Yes. On a day neither of us feels obliged to win.\"\n\n\"Then we may finally learn something.\"\n\nI leaned closer, my answer to the invitation plain in the way my fingers curled around his. The kiss waited until the game was done. That made it feel less like a prize and more like something we had both wanted all along.\n\nI wanted to kiss the smile off his face, which was inconvenient because he was doing his best to win. The moment made me think of the first time I'd sat in this room and mistaken his warm patience for the absence of loneliness. He could be wise and silly and competitive. I liked the silly part more than I'd expected.\n\nThe board grew crowded. Iroh could have won in three moves if I'd followed his usual pattern. Instead I traded a piece he considered valuable for a route he'd left open.\n\nHe stared.\n\n\"Did you mean to do that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"You gave up the center.\"\n\n\"I wanted the outside.\"\n\nHe leaned back, then laughed properly. \"You are very difficult to teach.\"\n\n\"Try being loved by me.\"\n\n\"I am.\"\n\nI felt heat in my cheeks. \"That's not fair. You can't say something like that when I'm trying to concentrate.\"\n\n\"Perhaps I have developed a strategy after all.\"\n\nWe finished the game. I won by a narrow margin, and he made me recount the move that had changed it. He listened as though it mattered. I didn't need him to pretend to lose, and I didn't need to win to be sure he enjoyed my company.\n\nAs we packed the tiles, he asked about the people I would visit next week.\n\n\"I'll be going through the Threadway on Thursday,\" I said. \"I've already told you the days. Do you want a note when I arrive?\"\n\n\"Only if you wish to send one. I would like to know you're safe. I do not need an account of every person you see.\"\n\nI nodded. Our agreement about ethical non-monogamy and my preference for polyamory wasn't a single confession that solved everything. It was a pattern of choices, like returning to a game after an argument and discovering you could still surprise each other.\n\nIroh held out the last tile. \"One more round?\"\n\n\"Are you prepared to lose again?\"\n\n\"Not in the slightest.\"\n\nI set the box aside. \"Then I'm going to claim my prize first.\"\n\n\"And what is that?\"\n\n\"A kiss, if you want one.\"\n\nHis smile changed. \"Very much.\"\n\nHe met me halfway across the table. We bumped the kettle with our elbows and had to stop laughing before we tried again. The kiss that followed was quiet enough to make the laughter seem far away.\n\nLater we went upstairs and closed the door. The board remained on the table, unfinished for the next game. In the morning, I found the white lotus tile at the center and a note in Iroh's careful hand: *A rematch is requested. No tricks promised.*\n\nI put it in my pocket and went downstairs to help open the shop.","word_count":1276},"avatar-30":{"number":30,"title":"The Fire Beneath the Kettle","content":"The campfire was small enough that I could have stepped over it.\n\nIroh sat before it as though he were looking into a room full of people. The kettle hung from a short iron hook. Rain had stopped an hour earlier, and the wood was damp around the edges.\n\n\"You've been staring at that flame for ten minutes,\" I said.\n\n\"Only eight.\"\n\n\"You counted?\"\n\n\"I was hoping you would notice.\"\n\nI sat on the dry side of the blanket. We'd spent the day following the upper river road toward Ba Sing Se, carrying our packs and arguing about the best place to buy breakfast. The town we'd stayed in was behind us. Ahead, the road bent along a stand of trees to a small travelers' shelter where we intended to spend the night.\n\nI had asked whether he would show me firebending as something other than a weapon.\n\nIt sounded romantic when I said it.\n\nIt became much less romantic when I realized I knew almost nothing about fire safety.\n\n\"Before we begin,\" he said, \"what would you like to learn?\"\n\n\"Why you treat a cooking fire as though it has opinions.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\n\"An excellent question.\"\n\n\"Also how you can warm tea without setting everything on fire.\"\n\n\"Another excellent question. I suspect this one will be more practical.\"\n\nHe began with the ordinary work. We cleared the wet leaves from around the stones. He tested the kettle hook. I brought water from the stream and filtered it through the camp cloth as he'd shown me earlier in the trip. Neither of us treated bending as a substitute for making sure the pot could stand safely.\n\nThen he held his hands near the flame.\n\n\"Fire is easy to fear when all you have seen is what it can destroy,\" he said. \"But look at what we ask this one to do.\"\n\n\"Boil water.\"\n\n\"Yes. Warm two travelers. Dry a pair of very unhappy socks.\"\n\n\"They're your socks.\"\n\n\"Then their unhappiness is particularly severe.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe breathed in slowly, then let a thin ribbon of fire brighten in the hearth without climbing beyond the stones. He did not strike a heroic pose. He moved with the care of someone whose body had learned both force and restraint.\n\nI felt the warmth rise against my face.\n\n\"That isn't a trick,\" I said.\n\n\"No. And it isn't a command to the fire to obey a more important man. It is something I practice with respect.\"\n\nI watched the flames settle.\n\n\"Can someone learn that without bending?\"\n\n\"You can learn to tend a flame, understand its fuel and air, recognize when it has become unsafe. Bending is one way of working with fire, not the only way of caring for it.\"\n\nI liked that answer.\n\nA traveler with a cart arrived while the kettle warmed. She had been delayed by a washed-out patch of road and asked whether the shelter had room for another bedroll. Iroh stood and offered her space beside the far wall.\n\nShe looked at the fire.\n\n\"Will it stay small?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he answered. \"And if you would prefer it out while you sleep, tell us.\"\n\nThe traveler thanked him and settled her bag.\n\nI expected him to keep talking about firebending. Instead he helped her find a dry place for her boots and asked whether she had enough water. She did. She asked for nothing more.\n\nWhen he sat beside me again, I said, \"You didn't perform for her.\"\n\n\"She wanted shelter, not a demonstration.\"\n\n\"That may be the most important part of your lesson.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThe kettle began to hum.\n\nIroh lifted it from the hook and let the fire ease back to ordinary embers. We poured tea into two cups. He tasted his and looked pleased.\n\n\"Would you like to know what I once believed?\" he asked.\n\nI nodded, but I didn't move closer until he made room.\n\n\"When I was young, I was praised for power that frightened people. It is possible to become very good at something before you ask whether you should be proud of the way you use it.\"\n\nI looked toward the dark trees.\n\n\"Do you miss being powerful?\"\n\nHe was quiet for a long moment.\n\nIroh checked the fire once more and asked me to read the warning he'd written in the travel notebook.\n\n\"Small flames can still travel,\" I read. \"Clear the ground. Keep water close. Never assume a bending technique replaces a watch.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Are you teaching me, or making me supervise you?\"\n\n\"Both, apparently.\"\n\nHe laughed. His shoulders relaxed when he looked at the kettle. There was something tender in the way he treated a controlled flame as a living thing that could offer warmth without becoming a weapon.\n\nA traveler approached the shelter carrying wet boots. We made room without asking for their story. Iroh offered tea; the traveler asked for hot water instead and sat at the far end of the bench.\n\n\"Perfectly reasonable,\" Iroh murmured after he returned.\n\n\"You look disappointed.\"\n\n\"I am. I make excellent tea.\"\n\n\"Sometimes people just want warm feet.\"\n\nHe considered that, then passed the hot water over without another suggestion. The traveler thanked him and returned to drying their boots.\n\nI watched the little exchange and thought about how often Iroh had learned to help by becoming less certain that he knew the best way.\n\n\"Can we walk tomorrow without any lessons?\" I asked.\n\n\"Certainly. Though I may accidentally teach you about a tree.\"\n\n\"One tree, maximum.\"\n\nHe bowed gravely. \"An impossible restriction.\"\n\nI laughed and leaned against his shoulder. He waited before touching my hand, and I appreciated the quiet asking. There were people I loved in other places, and I had already told him that ethical non-monogamy and polyamory mattered to me. Sitting here with him wasn't less tender because the rest of my life still existed.\n\nThe fire settled into safe embers. Nobody was watching us for guidance. It was the best part of the evening.\n\n\"I have not stopped being powerful,\" he said. \"I have stopped treating every situation as an invitation to demonstrate it.\"\n\nThe distinction made me look at him differently.\n\nHe still carried the old general's posture. He still noticed where every traveler sat, how quickly somebody reached for a bag, whether a door could be closed from the inside. Those habits might protect people or make them feel watched, depending on what he did with them.\n\n\"Do you want me to tell you when that posture makes me nervous?\" I asked.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\n\"I did get nervous when you checked all the exits in the inn yesterday.\"\n\nHe closed his eyes.\n\n\"I am sorry. I was worried about the rain and made no explanation.\"\n\n\"You don't have to explain every thought. But I'd rather you tell me you're checking the roof than let me imagine you're waiting for an attack.\"\n\nHe opened his eyes.\n\n\"That is a fair request.\"\n\nThe traveler at the far end of the shelter had begun to snore, very gently.\n\nI tried not to laugh.\n\nIroh heard it, glanced toward her, and smiled.\n\n\"This is what I wished to learn,\" he said.\n\n\"How to ignore snoring?\"\n\n\"How to be in a place without needing to decide its fate.\"\n\nI held my cup between my hands. We had discussed my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory before making romance part of our journey. Iroh didn't need to become my sole refuge. Nor did I need to be the person who repaired everything his past had broken.\n\n\"Can I ask you one more question?\" I said.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"If I tell you I love spending time with you, will you hear it as a request to become someone different?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"And if I tell you I value this moment very much, will you hear it as a demand that you never travel elsewhere?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe offered his hand.\n\nI took it.\n\nThe kettle had cooled. The fire was low, but the stones still held warmth. I moved closer and kissed his cheek, then paused.\n\n\"May I?\"\n\nHis smile was soft.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nWe kissed slowly beneath the shelter roof. Somewhere beyond the trees, water moved over the river stones. The traveler slept on, uninterested in our private lives.\n\nLater we banked the embers and checked that the fire was safe. Iroh put the kettle away; I hung his unhappy socks where they might dry by morning.\n\nBefore sleep, I wrote a line in my notebook.\n\n*Power was not the part I loved. It was the care he chose when nobody was asking him to win.*\n\nIn the morning, the socks were still damp.\n\nIroh declared this a grave injustice.\n\nWe laughed over breakfast, packed the kettle, and continued toward the city without lighting any heroic flames.","word_count":1470},"avatar-31":{"number":31,"title":"A Letter from the Palace","content":"Zuko's letter arrived during the morning tea service, when Iroh had both hands full of cups.\n\nThe courier set the sealed packet on the counter and asked for a receipt. Mina looked at the Fire Nation crest, then at the customers waiting for their orders.\n\n\"I can sign for it,\" I offered.\n\n\"Please,\" Iroh said. \"If I set these down, the jasmine will go bitter.\"\n\nHis voice was light. The way he watched the packet was not.\n\nWe finished the orders before opening it. An older customer wanted less hot water; another asked why the price of mountain tea had increased. Mina explained the supplier's shortage and offered the ordinary house blend. There was no royal decree that could change what the delivery cart carried.\n\nOnly after the last customer had gone did Iroh take the packet into the courtyard.\n\nHe sat beneath the climbing vine, opened the letter and read it twice.\n\n\"Zuko?\" I asked.\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Is he well?\"\n\n\"As well as anyone can be who discovers each morning that a throne is a very uncomfortable chair.\"\n\nI smiled. \"What does he want?\"\n\n\"Advice.\"\n\n\"About?\"\n\n\"A coastal warehouse once used for military stores. The port workers want space for cargo; displaced families want shelter. The merchants say they cannot operate if the building is taken from trade. Everyone has made an argument that is at least partly true.\"\n\n\"What does Zuko ask?\"\n\nIroh read the last line aloud: \"How do I know whether a difficult decision is right when everyone will be disappointed?\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"That's a hard question.\"\n\n\"I had hoped to provide an easy answer.\"\n\n\"Would an easy answer be honest?\"\n\nHe folded the letter with unnecessary precision.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nWe stayed there for a while. Iroh watched a pair of birds argue over the courtyard wall. I could almost see the old general turning the warehouse into a map of competing forces: one side here, another there, a line that could be held.\n\n\"What do you know about the building?\" I asked.\n\n\"Very little.\"\n\n\"Then what could you tell him without deciding for the people who live there?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"That the workers should be heard, and the families. That neither needs to be treated as a problem obstructing his wisdom.\"\n\n\"That's a start.\"\n\nI took out my notebook and drew two columns. One held things Iroh had learned from mistakes. The other held questions only the people at the harbor could answer: the floor's condition, seasonal storms, who could safely use the building, what it would cost to maintain.\n\nIroh examined the columns.\n\n\"Are you writing a report on my ignorance?\"\n\n\"A very affectionate report.\"\n\n\"With dreadful handwriting.\"\n\n\"That too.\"\n\nHe laughed, then grew quiet.\n\n\"I miss him,\" he said.\n\n\"Zuko?\"\n\n\"Yes. I miss being close enough to see when he has gone without sleep. But I do not miss the palace, the way every kindness becomes an obligation someone can invoke later.\"\n\n\"Would you like to visit?\"\n\n\"Sometimes. Not as a minister who never returns home.\"\n\n\"Then say that.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Can one write to a Fire Lord and offer help without offering one's entire life?\"\n\n\"Apparently we're about to find out.\"\n\nHe reached for the pen.\n\nI stopped him gently.\n\n\"Before you write, can we talk about tonight?\"\n\nHe frowned. \"What about tonight?\"\n\n\"We planned dinner together. You've been postponing sleep whenever a letter from the palace arrives. I'm not asking you to ignore Zuko. I'm asking whether you'd like to answer him tomorrow instead.\"\n\nThe expression on his face was almost painful.\n\n\"He needs me.\"\n\n\"I know. And he also needs to know that he can choose something with the people he's responsible for. You can't live every day on both sides of the world.\"\n\nIroh watched the seal.\n\n\"You make an old man's usefulness sound very limited.\"\n\n\"No. I make it sound like a choice.\"\n\nIroh opened a fresh sheet and began his reply to Zuko, then stopped.\n\n\"I was going to tell him what to do.\"\n\n\"Does he need an order?\"\n\n\"No. He asked whether I could advise.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps write what you've learned and ask what he's already tried.\"\n\nIroh smiled faintly. \"You make it sound so reasonable.\"\n\n\"It isn't easier. It just leaves him room to choose.\"\n\nHe wrote about the limits of what he knew from a distance. The old supply route in Zuko's letter had been damaged by bad weather, but Iroh couldn't honestly prescribe a repair based on a sketch and a few anxious lines. He could suggest who might examine it and offer to talk when Zuko wanted.\n\n\"I want him safe,\" he said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I remember being the man who could order an army, and I think I should be able to make one nephew safe.\"\n\nI touched the edge of the paper. \"You can love him without turning him into another command.\"\n\nThe words cost me something too. I'd caught myself hoping Iroh would be equally decisive about my own worries, that his wisdom would spare me the work of choosing. He wasn't a shelter I could simply move into and stop being responsible for my life.\n\nHe sealed the letter only after reading it through, then showed me the final line because he wanted to: *Tell me what you need, not what you think I expect you to need.*\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"That sounds like a good question for the shop too.\"\n\nHe glanced at Mina through the open door. She was counting stock and pretending she hadn't been listening.\n\n\"She has been asking me that question for years,\" he said.\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"I am a slow student.\"\n\nWe laughed. He put the sealed letter with the courier's papers rather than holding it hostage until he felt less worried. The shop clock chimed the hour, and Iroh went to sign the delivery invoice he'd promised Mina before closing.\n\nHe sat back.\n\nMina appeared at the courtyard door with a delivery invoice. Two jars of citrus peel had been billed as three. Iroh glanced at the letter, then took the invoice.\n\n\"This I can solve before dinner,\" he said.\n\nHe asked Mina to compare the delivery sheet with the actual jars. They found the arithmetic error and drafted a note to the supplier. No magic, no bending, no palace messenger; just an ordinary business that would otherwise have paid for something it hadn't received.\n\nBefore closing, Iroh asked Mina whether she would prefer the late shift on Thursday.\n\n\"Why?\" she asked.\n\n\"I have a date.\"\n\nMina looked at me, then back at him.\n\n\"That is not a reason to rearrange my week without asking.\"\n\n\"Precisely why I'm asking.\"\n\nShe checked the schedule and offered to trade one evening for a morning shift she wanted free. Iroh wrote down the exchange and confirmed her hours would remain paid.\n\n\"That is the most romantic thing I've heard from you all day,\" I said.\n\n\"Paying staff fairly?\"\n\n\"Knowing their lives don't vanish because ours are busy.\"\n\nMina hid a smile as she put the ledger away.\n\nIroh looked at the table where Zuko's letter waited.\n\n\"That, too, is something I ought to remember when advising my nephew,\" he said.\n\nBy closing time Iroh seemed less tense.\n\nHe made dinner. We argued over how much ginger counted as an unreasonable amount.\n\n\"It is a philosophical matter,\" he told me.\n\n\"It is a question of whether the soup still tastes like soup.\"\n\n\"All philosophy begins with a bowl.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nAfter we ate, he took a fresh sheet of paper.\n\n\"I would like to write a short answer,\" he said. \"Would you sit nearby without correcting my every sentence?\"\n\n\"That's going to require discipline.\"\n\n\"From both of us.\"\n\nHe began by telling Zuko he loved him. He suggested inviting the workers, families and merchants to explain their needs in their own words and checking the building before promising it to anyone. He offered to visit at an agreed time if his nephew wished.\n\nHe stopped before adding a fourth paragraph.\n\n\"Enough?\" I asked.\n\n\"Probably.\"\n\nI looked at the letter, then at him.\n\n\"We're getting better at knowing when to stop.\"\n\n\"On rare occasions.\"\n\nWe had already spoken openly about my being ethically non-monogamous and preferring polyamory. Iroh understood that loving him did not mean ending every other relationship. The same principle mattered here: I did not want him to have to abandon his nephew to belong with me, or abandon his own life to be a good uncle.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\" I asked.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"I had rather hoped you would.\"\n\nWe kissed beneath the vine, slow and warm. When we drew apart he admitted the soup had contained too much ginger. I laughed against his cheek.\n\nThe letter rested on the table while we went upstairs. We closed the door and kept the rest of the night to ourselves.\n\nIn the morning he handed it to the courier without demanding a promise that Zuko would follow his advice.\n\nThen he opened the shop on time.\n\nMina pointed at the stove.\n\n\"It still needs repair.\"\n\nIroh sighed.\n\n\"An enemy I know how to face.\"\n\nI fetched the tools.","word_count":1526}}};for(const s of lib.series){const e=revisions[s.series_slug]||{};for(const c of s.chapters){const r=e[c.id];if(!r||c.chapter_number!==r.number)continue;c.content=r.content;c.word_count=r.word_count;c.title=r.title;}}})();
